result:
Fixed. The abstract-name path was passing the whole `OsStr` bytes — including the leading NUL sentinel — to `std::os::unix::net::SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`, which expects the name *without* that byte and prepends the NUL itself. The result was a socket registered as `\0\0aaa` instead of `\0aaa`.

Changed both call sites to strip the sentinel first:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:82` (`UnixListener::bind`)
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:78` (`UnixStream::connect`)

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
// `from_abstract_name` expects the name without the leading nul
// byte that marks the path as an abstract socket address.
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Since bind and connect were mangling the name identically, the pair still found each other — which is why the existing `abstract_socket_name` test passed before; only the round-tripped address exposed it. `cargo test --features full --test uds_stream` now passes all 8 tests including the `as_abstract_name() == b"aaa"` assertion, and `uds_datagram`/`uds_split`/`uds_cred` are green.
